{"model": "claude-fable-5", "id": "msg_011CdEeHZb3yee3cCrrYUQ8o", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}, {"type": "text", "text": "## Part 1: The structural portrait, from first principles\n\nLet F : \u2102\u207f \u2192 \u2102\u207f be polynomial with det JF a nonzero constant, and suppose F is *not* invertible. Squeeze everything we can out of these two hypotheses before choosing a search space.\n\n**1. F is \u00e9tale everywhere.** Constant nonzero Jacobian means F is a local analytic isomorphism at every point of \u2102\u207f. There are no critical points and no ramification anywhere in affine space. Whatever goes wrong cannot go wrong locally.\n\n**2. F must be non-injective, with geometric degree d \u2265 2.** If F were injective, it would be surjective (Ax\u2013Grothendieck), and an injective \u00e9tale endomorphism of \u2102\u207f is an automorphism with polynomial inverse. So a counterexample *is* precisely a non-injective \u00e9tale endomorphism: there exist a \u2260 b with F(a) = F(b), and the field extension \u2102(x)/\u2102(F) has degree d \u2265 2, so the generic fiber has exactly d points (all simple \u2014 no multiplicity, by \u00e9taleness).\n\n**3. F cannot be proper \u2014 all pathology is hidden at infinity.** An \u00e9tale *proper* map onto \u2102\u207f would be a d-sheeted covering of a simply connected space, hence d = 1. So non-properness is forced: there are sequences x\u2096 \u2192 \u221e with F(x\u2096) convergent. The non-properness locus A(F) (the Jelonek set) is nonempty, of pure codimension 1, and is swept out by images of curves going to infinity (it is \u2102*-uniruled). Any values F misses lie inside A(F).\n\n**4. Away from A(F), F is an honest covering.** F restricted to \u2102\u207f \\ F\u207b\u00b9(A) \u2192 \u2102\u207f \\ A is a genuine d-sheeted covering map. So a counterexample is equivalent to: *an exotic finite cover of the complement of a hypersurface A \u2282 \u2102\u207f, whose total space is again affine space minus a hypersurface, and which \"loses sheets to infinity\" over A rather than ramifying.* This imposes brutal constraints:\n- \u03c0\u2081(\u2102\u207f \\ A) must admit an index-d subgroup whose cover has the homology and rationality of a hypersurface complement in \u2102\u207f. Complements of generic or mildly singular hypersurfaces (e.g., nodal curves, by Deligne\u2013Fulton) have *abelian* fundamental groups, and the abelian covers can be analyzed and largely excluded. So A must be highly singular or in very special position.\n- Compactify and resolve at infinity: F extends to a proper map from some rational compactification X\u0304 \u2192 \u2119\u207f, ramified *only along boundary divisors*, and the sheets lost over A come from **dicritical divisors at infinity** whose images are exactly the components of A. Riemann\u2013Hurwitz at infinity plus Euler-characteristic additivity (1 = d\u00b7\u03c7(\u2102\u207f \\ A) + \u03c7(F\u207b\u00b9(A)), with fiber counts over strata of A) yields hard Diophantine constraints tying d to the topology of A.\n\n**5. Where this points the search.** In n = 2 the accumulated constraints are crushing: both component degrees > 100 (Moh-type verification), Newton polygons of the pair must be similar, generic fibers need \u2265 2 places at infinity (Abhyankar\u2013Moh), and low sheet numbers are excluded (Orevkov/Domrina). So in the plane, *coefficient search is hopeless; only combinatorial search over infinity-data is viable.* Conversely, degree 2 is always fine (Wang), and stabilization trades degree for dimension \u2014 so the natural coefficient-search stratum is **low degree (3), high dimension**, i.e., F = x + H with H cubic homogeneous and JH nilpotent (Bass\u2013Connell\u2013Wright/Yagzhev), or Dru\u017ckowski form F = x + (Ax)^\u22183. These reductions aren't just tradition; the first-principles picture says the pathology lives in global linear-algebraic structure, and this stratum makes that structure searchable.\n\n## Part 2: Primary strategy\n\nTwo prongs, both dictated by the portrait: the **double-point locus** is the object to hunt (prong A), and the **infinity data** is the object to design (prong B).\n\n**Prong A (forward, algebraic): hunt for double points in the Dru\u017ckowski stratum.**\nFor F(x) = x + (Ax)^\u22183, setting v = a \u2212 b, u = Aa, w = Ab, one computes\nF(a) \u2212 F(b) = (I + D\u00b7A)v, where D = diag(u\u1d62\u00b2 + u\u1d62w\u1d62 + w\u1d62\u00b2).\nSo *a counterexample exists in this stratum iff* there is a matrix A satisfying the Keller/nilpotency identities (which say I + 3\u00b7diag((Ax)^\u22182)\u00b7A is unimodular for **all** x \u2014 note this is exactly the u = w specialization, D = diag(3u\u1d62\u00b2)) *while* some genuinely polarized D (u \u2260 w, with Av = u \u2212 w) makes I + DA singular. The counterexample lives precisely in the gap between the \"diagonal squares\" cone and the \"polarized cubes\" cone. This is a concrete incidence variety in (A, a, b)-space: nilpotency equations + det(I + DA) = 0 + v \u2260 0. Known verifications cover small n and small rank(A), so target n \u2248 10\u201314, rank(A) \u2265 5, quotient by the GL-conjugation symmetry, and attack with numerical algebraic geometry (irreducible decomposition, dimension bookkeeping to find parameter ranges where the incidence variety *could* have components not forced into the diagonal).\n\n**Prong B (backward, geometric): design the infinity data first.**\nEnumerate candidate asymptotic configurations: a hypersurface A (curve in the n = 2 lab, surface in \u2102\u00b3), a degree d, dicritical divisor data, and boundary resolution graphs satisfying the Riemann\u2013Hurwitz-at-infinity and Euler characteristic Diophantine constraints, plus the \u03c0\u2081 index-d subgroup requirement (computed via Zariski\u2013van Kampen / braid monodromy). Every surviving combinatorial configuration becomes a structured realization problem: solve for coefficients of F with prescribed Puiseux behavior along the curves at infinity. This is exactly the style of analysis that produced the exclusion results in the plane \u2014 the point of automating it is to push past the published bounds and, in n = 3, into completely uncharted territory where no exclusions exist at all.\n\n## Part 3: First concrete steps\n\n1. **Build the verification stack** (Macaulay2/Singular + HomotopyContinuation.jl):\n - Keller check: det JF \u2261 const, symbolically.\n - Invertibility test: Gr\u00f6bner elimination on \u27e8F\u1d62(x) \u2212 y\u1d62\u27e9; F is an automorphism iff x\u1d62 reduce to polynomials in y (with the known degree bound deg F\u207b\u00b9 \u2264 (deg F)^(n\u22121), this is a *decidable* finite check).\n - Geometric degree estimator: reduce mod several large primes p, take random y \u2208 \ud835\udd3d\u209a\u207f, count fiber points via Gr\u00f6bner. **Master filter: Keller + generic fiber size \u2265 2 over char 0 \u21d2 counterexample.** The mod-p count is the cheap proxy; confirm any hit in characteristic 0.\n - Jelonek set computation (Jelonek's algorithm is effective) and numerical fiber tracking: follow the d fiber points as y crosses candidate components of A and watch which sheets escape to infinity.\n\n2. **Calibrate on a corpus with known answers:** tame automorphisms, the Nagata map, Pinchuk's real counterexample (the closest existing object to the portrait \u2014 dissect its complex geometry: where its Jacobian vanishes over \u2102, what its Jelonek set looks like, how the sheets escape), char-p Frobenius-type counterexamples, and the verified Dru\u017ckowski families. The pipeline must classify all of these correctly before I trust it on unknowns.\n\n3. **Run the Prong-A dimension audit** before any blind search: for each (n, rank) compute expected dimensions of the nilpotency variety and the double-point incidence variety; search only where an excess intersection is numerically plausible. Then run monodromy-based numerical irreducible decomposition on those systems.\n\n4. **Start the Prong-B enumerator in n = 2** (as a lab, reproducing and extending the d \u2264 4 exclusions), then port the framework to toric compactifications of \u2102\u00b3, where the first genuinely new candidate configurations should appear.\n\n5. **Maintain a \"near-miss\" library:** every configuration that fails for an identifiable reason (\u03c0\u2081 abelian, RH bookkeeping off by a fixed amount, double-point component collapsing to the diagonal) gets logged. If the conjecture is true, these failure patterns are data toward a proof; if false, the counterexample will sit where the failure reasons run out \u2014 and the library tells you where that is.\n\nThe one-sentence summary of the philosophy: a counterexample is not a random polynomial map \u2014 it is an \u00e9tale self-cover of \u2102\u207f whose entire topology is smuggled out to infinity along dicritical divisors over a very special hypersurface, so search the space of *infinity configurations* and *double-point loci*, never the raw coefficient space."}], "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 188, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 13177, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 9972}, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global"}}